Read out of the code while reviewing the test fix for #143. NOT verified
against a running foundation -- the reasoning is from the source, so
reproduce it before fixing.

lib/App/karr/Foundation/Runner.pm, _run_command:

    my $pid = fork;
    user_error("fork failed: $!") unless defined $pid;

    if ( $pid == 0 ) {
      close $reader;
      chdir "$repo" or die "chdir $repo: $!";
      open( STDOUT, '>&', $writer ) or die "dup stdout: $!";
      open( STDERR, '>&STDOUT' )    or die "dup stderr: $!";
      exec( '/bin/sh', '-c', $command ) or die "exec: $!";
    }

    # parent
    close $writer;
    open( my $log_fh, '>>', "$log_file" ) or user_error("open log $log_file: $!");

The child is already forked and, in the normal case, has already exec'd
the agent command. If the parent's `open log` then fails, user_error
croaks and _run_command never reaches its waitpid -- so the parent
abandons a running agent process. That is #143's cause seen from the
library side rather than the test side: the test could not reap what the
library dropped.

Whether it matters depends on what catches that croak. If it terminates
the whole karr-foundation run, init reaps and the damage is one orphaned
agent command still writing into a pipe nobody reads. If the drain loop
catches it per repo and moves to the next board, every affected board
leaves another one behind for the lifetime of the run.

Two things to decide:

- reap (or kill) the child on any failure between fork and waitpid. The
  log open is the only one today, but the shape invites more.
- whether an unopenable log should abort the run for that repo at all
  once a child is already executing. Starting the agent and then refusing
  to watch it is the worst of the three options; the other two are
  refusing to start it (check the log before forking) or running it with
  logging degraded.

Checking the log file is openable BEFORE the fork would remove the window
entirely and is probably the smaller change.
Fixed in 2968651 -- but the ticket as filed was half wrong, and the reachable half was somewhere else. The log-open window I described needs a genuine TOCTOU race to reach: _append_log('START ...') twenty lines earlier opens the same file in the same mode via Path::Tiny and croaks first on every stable cause (EISDIR/EACCES/EROFS/ENOENT), and EMFILE is ruled out because the parent closes the pipe's write end immediately before. The defect that needs no race is the TIMEOUT _append_log between the read loop and the SIGTERM/SIGKILL/waitpid: an agent that removes or replaces .karr.log during its own up-to-30-minute run takes that append down and skips the only thing that stops it. Reproduced full-chain over two boards: run() returns 0, one live unwatched agent per board, both .karr.lock files released -- so the next tick starts a second agent on top. My guess about the drain loop catching per repo was right (Foundation.pm:286 and :451).
